```

**Current behavior**
I'm not sure this is a bug. If it is, please fix. If not, please clarify the documentation OR (preferrably imo) change the behavior.

This works (no exception thrown)
```typescript
@Injectable()
class MySvc {
  constructor(cdr:ChangeDetectorRef) {}
}

@Component({
  ...
  providers: [MySvc]
}) 
class MyComponent {
  constructor() {m:MySvc}
}
```

...whereas this doesn't (it throws a NoProviderError)

```typescript
// app.module.ts
@NgModule({
  ...,
  providers: [{
    provide: MyService,
    deps: [ChangeDetectorRef],
    useFactory: cdr => new MyService(cdr)
  }]
})

// my-component.ts
@Component({...}) 
class MyComponent {
  constructor() {m:MySvc}
}
```

Furthermore, this results in an error (which also seems surprising)

```typescript
@Component({...})
class MyComponent {
  constructor(inj:Injector, cdRef:ChangeDetectorRef) {
    inj.get(ChangeDetectorRef);
  }
}
```

**Expected behavior**
I would expect all these things to work. But it seems `ChangeDetectorRef` (and other types close to view creation?) are special. They aren't injected normally and sometimes work and sometimes not, with very surprising results. 

So wish list is, in priority order:
1. Make changes so that these examples actually work, by always providing the `ChangeDetectorRef` (and potentially other injectables) to the component injector.
2. Document which classes are special and why

**What is the motivation / use case for changing the behavior?**
I, and others, find it strange and run into confusing behavior.

* **Angular version:** 
2.4.2
